(A short handbook for people who want to brew better coffee at home. It covers what happens) Tj T*
(during) Tj T*
(extraction, how to set the ratio of coffee to water, how grind size and water temperature) Tj T*
(change) Tj T*
(the cup, and how to taste your results.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1 Extraction) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1.1 What Extraction Means) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Brewing coffee is extraction: hot water dissolves soluble compounds from ground coffee.) Tj T*
(Roughly 30%) Tj T*
(of the mass of a roasted bean can dissolve in water, but a pleasant cup extracts only) Tj T*
(about 18 to) Tj T*
(22% of the dry coffee. Acids dissolve first, then sugars, and the bitter, woody compounds) Tj T*
(come last.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When too little is extracted \(under-extraction\), the cup tastes sour, thin and salty. When) Tj T*
(too much) Tj T*
(is extracted \(over-extraction\), it tastes bitter, hollow and drying. The goal is the) Tj T*
(balanced middle,) Tj T*
(where the sweetness of the sugars covers the acidity and the bitterness stays in the) Tj T*
(background.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1.2 Strength Is Not Extraction) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Strength describes how concentrated the drink is: how much dissolved coffee ends up in the) Tj T*
(cup. It) Tj T*
(is measured as total dissolved solids \(TDS\). Filter coffee usually tastes best between) Tj T*
(1.15% and) Tj T*
(1.35% TDS. Strength and extraction are separate: a strong cup can still be) Tj T*
(under-extracted, and a) Tj T*
(weak cup can be over-extracted. You change strength mostly with the ratio of coffee to) Tj T*
(water, and) Tj T*
(extraction mostly with grind size, temperature and contact time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
