(2.1 The Brew Ratio) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The brew ratio is the weight of coffee compared to the weight of water. A ratio of 1:16) Tj T*
(means one) Tj T*
(gram of coffee for every sixteen grams of water. Most filter recipes sit between 1:15 and) Tj T*
(1:17;) Tj T*
(1:16 is a good starting point. A lower number \(1:15\) gives a stronger cup, a higher number) Tj T*
(\(1:17\) a) Tj T*
(lighter one.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Weigh both coffee and water with a kitchen scale. Measuring by volume with spoons is) Tj T*
(unreliable,) Tj T*
(because a spoonful of a light roast weighs more than a spoonful of a dark roast.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2.2 Calculating A Dose) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To find the water for a dose, multiply the coffee weight by the ratio. For 20 g of coffee) Tj T*
(at 1:16 you) Tj T*
(need 320 g of water \(20 x 16\). To brew a 500 g pot at 1:16, divide instead: 500 / 16) Tj T*
(gives about) Tj T*
(31 g of coffee. One gram of water is one millilitre, so the scale reading in grams equals) Tj T*
(the volume) Tj T*
(in millilitres.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3 Grind size) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3.1 Why Grind Size Matters) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Grinding exposes the inside of the bean to water. A finer grind has more surface area, so) Tj T*
(water) Tj T*
(extracts faster; a coarser grind extracts more slowly. Grind size also controls how fast) Tj T*
(water flows) Tj T*
(through a filter: fine grounds slow the flow and lengthen the contact time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As a rule of thumb, adjust grind size first when a cup tastes off. If it is sour, grind) Tj T*
(finer. If it) Tj T*
(is bitter, grind coarser. Change one thing at a time so you can taste the effect.) Tj T*
ET
