(3.2 Matching Grind To Method) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Each brewing method suits a range of grind sizes. Espresso needs a very fine grind, like) Tj T*
(flour. A) Tj T*
(pour-over cone such as a V60 works with a medium-fine grind, similar to table salt. A) Tj T*
(French press,) Tj T*
(where coffee steeps for about four minutes, needs a coarse grind, like sea salt, so that) Tj T*
(the metal) Tj T*
(filter can hold the grounds back. A burr grinder gives more even particles than a blade) Tj T*
(grinder,) Tj T*
(and even particles extract evenly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4 Water) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4.1 Temperature) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Water between 90 and 96 degC extracts coffee well. Water just off the boil, around 96 degC,) Tj T*
(suits light) Tj T*
(roasts, which are dense and harder to extract. Dark roasts extract easily and can taste) Tj T*
(harsh with) Tj T*
(very hot water, so 90 to 92 degC works better for them. Without a thermometer, let boiled) Tj T*
(water rest) Tj T*
(for about 30 seconds before pouring.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4.2 Water Quality) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A cup of filter coffee is about 98% water, so the water itself matters. Use clean,) Tj T*
(odour-free water.) Tj T*
(Very soft water can make coffee taste flat, while very hard water dulls acidity and leaves) Tj T*
(scale in) Tj T*
(kettles. Filtered tap water is a good choice in most places.) Tj T*
