(Start by pouring about twice the weight of the coffee in water, for example 40 g of water) Tj T*
(on 20 g of) Tj T*
(coffee, and wait 30 to 45 seconds. Freshly roasted coffee releases carbon dioxide when it) Tj T*
(gets wet,) Tj T*
(and the bed of coffee bubbles and rises. This is called the bloom. Letting the gas escape) Tj T*
(first helps) Tj T*
(the rest of the water extract the coffee evenly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5.2 Pouring And Timing) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After the bloom, pour the rest of the water slowly in circles, keeping the bed evenly wet.) Tj T*
(For a) Tj T*
(single cup with 20 g of coffee and 320 g of water, the whole brew should take about three) Tj T*
(minutes.) Tj T*
(If it runs much faster, grind finer next time; if it runs much slower, grind coarser.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6 Tasting) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6.1 Tasting Your Cup) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let the coffee cool a little before tasting, because flavours are easier to notice when) Tj T*
(the cup is) Tj T*
(warm rather than hot. Notice three things: acidity \(bright, sour or flat\), sweetness and) Tj T*
(bitterness,) Tj T*
(and body \(how heavy the coffee feels in your mouth\). Write down the recipe and what you) Tj T*
(taste so you) Tj T*
(can repeat a good cup.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6.2 Adjusting The Next Brew) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Use what you taste to change one variable. Sour and thin points to under-extraction: grind) Tj T*
(finer or) Tj T*
(use hotter water. Bitter and drying points to over-extraction: grind coarser or use cooler) Tj T*
(water. If) Tj T*
(the cup is balanced but too weak or too strong, change the ratio rather than the grind.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
